7
00:00:24.179 --> 00:00:24.500
Knowledge Graphs are a very powerful,

8
00:00:33.159 --> 00:00:33.539
Knowledge Graphs provide a
way to store and organize data

9
00:00:39.890 --> 00:00:43.070
In contrast to traditional
relational databases which

10
00:00:43.070 --> 00:00:46.710
organize data into
tables with rows and columns.

11
00:00:47.429 --> 00:00:47.869
Knowledge Graphs instead
use a graph-based structure

12
00:01:11.260 --> 00:01:14.200
Each of these nodes or
edges in the graph can

13
00:01:14.200 --> 00:01:15.670
also store additional information about

14
00:01:15.670 --> 00:01:20.680
the entity in the case of the node or
the relationship in the case of the edge.

15
00:01:21.519 --> 00:01:24.810
For example, a person node
could store an individual's

16
00:01:24.810 --> 00:01:26.629
name, email, and other details.

17
00:01:27.290 --> 00:01:29.909
A company node could
store data like the number

18
00:01:29.909 --> 00:01:31.939
of employees, annual revenue, and so on.

19
00:01:32.540 --> 00:01:35.799
And the employer-employee
relationship between the company and the

20
00:01:35.799 --> 00:01:39.790
person would be represented by
an edge between these two nodes.

21
00:01:40.170 --> 00:01:43.069
And the edge could also
store additional information

22
00:01:43.069 --> 00:01:44.519
about that employment relationship,

23
00:01:44.519 --> 00:01:48.140
like the person's job
title, start date, and so on.

24
00:01:48.500 --> 00:01:53.939
The graph structure of nodes and
relationships is very flexible and lets you

25
00:01:53.939 --> 00:01:58.540
more conveniently model some parts of
the world than relational databases.

26
00:01:59.120 --> 00:01:59.840
That's right, Andrew.

27
00:02:00.700 --> 00:02:01.040
Knowledge Graphs make it much easier to
represent and search deep relationships

28
00:02:12.180 --> 00:02:14.289
This enables much
faster execution of queries,

29
00:02:14.289 --> 00:02:17.000
getting you to the data
you need more efficiently.

30
00:02:18.039 --> 00:02:20.860
This is why web search
engines and e-commerce

31
00:02:20.860 --> 00:02:22.969
sites that offer product search capability

32
00:02:22.969 --> 00:02:26.840
have found knowledge graphs a key
technology for delivering relevant results.

33
00:02:27.580 --> 00:02:31.389
In fact, if you search for, say,
a celebrity on Google or Bing,

34
00:02:31.389 --> 00:02:34.330
the results you get
back in the cards to the

35
00:02:34.330 --> 00:02:36.379
side are retrieved
using a knowledge graph.

36
00:02:36.740 --> 00:02:38.129
And when you combine a Knowledge
Graph with an embedding model,

37
00:02:44.960 --> 00:02:47.349
That's because you can
take advantage of the

38
00:02:47.349 --> 00:02:49.439
relationships and the
metadata stored in the

39
00:02:49.439 --> 00:02:52.120
graph to improve the
relevance of the text you

40
00:02:52.120 --> 00:02:54.210
retrieve and pass to the language model.

41
00:02:54.889 --> 00:02:58.099
In a basic retrieval
augmented generation or RAG system,

42
00:02:58.099 --> 00:03:01.310
the documents you want
to query or chat with

43
00:03:01.310 --> 00:03:03.810
might be first split into smaller sections

44
00:03:03.810 --> 00:03:04.169
or chunks which are then transformed
into vectors using an embedding model.

45
00:03:08.900 --> 00:03:10.340
Once in this vector form,

46
00:03:10.340 --> 00:03:13.800
you can use a similarity function
like cosine similarity to search through

47
00:03:13.800 --> 00:03:17.259
the chunks of text to find the
ones relevant to your prompt.

48
00:03:17.879 --> 00:03:21.840
But it turns out that storing
these text chunks in a knowledge draft

49
00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:25.199
opens up new ways to retrieve
relevant data from your documents.

50
00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:29.280
Rather than just similarity
search using text embeddings,

51
00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:31.330
you can retrieve one chunk, then,

52
00:03:39.349 --> 00:03:43.400
You see in this course how this
can review connections between

53
00:03:43.400 --> 00:03:45.979
text sources that
similarity-based RAAC can miss.

